<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Grauholz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-switzerland">French invasion of Switzerland</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Grauholz was fought on 5 March 1798 north-east of Bern, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-switzerland">French invasion of Switzerland</a>. A French army under General Schauenburg defeated the Bernese forces, and the city of Bern surrendered the same day. The defeat brought down the Old Swiss Confederacy, which was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-grauholz.jpg" alt="Battle of Grauholz" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Weberschiffchen at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GrauholzWalthard_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had already encroached on the associates of the Swiss Confederation, seeking control of the alpine passes and a buffer against the monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Part of the bishopric of Basel was absorbed in 1793, and in 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valtellina">Valtellina</a> to the new Cisalpine Republic and invaded the rest of the bishopric. In 1798 the republican faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-cesar-de-la-harpe">Frédéric-César de La Harpe</a>, invited the French in. Vaud was ruled by Bern, whose government often spoke another language, and when it revolted and declared a republic the French had a pretext to invade.</p><p>French troops entered the Lemanic Republic on 24 January 1798. Bern raised about 20,000 men and the rest of the Confederation 4,100, against about 35,000 French in two wings under Balthazar Alexis Henri Schauenburg. On 1 March the French invaded Bern and Solothurn, and on 2 March the battles of Lengnau, Twann, Grenchen and Bellach ended in French victories and the capitulation of Solothurn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Bernese army of about 6,400 men under Karl Ludwig von Erlach first met some 18,000 French at the Battle of Fraubrunnen and was driven back to Grauholz, a wooded hill in what are now the municipalities of Urtenen-Schönbühl and Moosseedorf. By then Erlach had about two battalions left. Many women, older men and even children of the countryside joined the fighting; an eyewitness described many people of the village of Bolligen coming to the army, and 27 of its farmers died. The Bernese held out for about two and a half hours before they broke and left the woods in small groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A report of 1804 put the Swiss losses at 2,000 and the French dead at about 1,600, while a later estimate gave Bern's total losses in the fighting of 2 to 5 March as about 700; French losses in the campaign are unknown. On the same day General Johann Rudolf von Graffenried defeated the southern French wing at Neuenegg, but the government's surrender and the defeat at Grauholz ended Bernese resistance. Erlach failed to hold the approaches to the city and set off for the Bernese Oberland to organise resistance, but at Wichtrach he was murdered by Bernese soldiers or farmers who believed him a traitor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Grauholz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In early 1798 the French Directory sent armies into the Confederacy; Vaud proclaimed itself free, and after the defeat of Bernese forces at the <b>Battle of Grauholz</b> on 5 March 1798, Bern surrendered and the old order collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French troops defeated Bern at <b>Grauholz</b> in 1798, and it was absorbed into the Helvetic Republic, losing Vaud and Aargau.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-bern">Canton of Bern</a></li></ul></section>
